<h2>Introduction to Medication Administration</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Medication administration is the process of safely preparing, giving, and documenting medications for a patient.

</p>


<p>

Medical assistants must understand medication safety, proper techniques, and legal responsibilities when assisting with medications.

</p>


<h3>Goals of Safe Medication Administration</h3>


<ul>

<li>Give the correct medication.</li>

<li>Prevent medication errors.</li>

<li>Protect patient safety.</li>

<li>Educate patients about their medications.</li>

<li>Document accurately.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Orders</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

A medication order is a written or electronic instruction from an authorized healthcare provider that allows medication to be administered.

</p>



<h3>Parts of a Medication Order</h3>


<ul>


<li><strong>Patient name:</strong> Identifies who receives the medication.</li>


<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> Shows when the order was written.</li>


<li><strong>Medication name:</strong> Identifies the drug.</li>


<li><strong>Dose:</strong> Amount of medication to administer.</li>


<li><strong>Route:</strong> How the medication enters the body.</li>


<li><strong>Frequency:</strong> How often medication is given.</li>


<li><strong>Provider signature:</strong> Authorizes the order.</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Labels</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Before administering medication, always compare the medication label with the provider's order.

</p>


<h3>Check the Label Three Times</h3>


<ol>


<li>When removing medication from storage.</li>


<li>When preparing the medication.</li>


<li>Before returning or administering the medication.</li>


</ol>



<h3>Check the Label For:</h3>


<ul>


<li>Medication name</li>

<li>Strength</li>

<li>Dose</li>

<li>Expiration date</li>

<li>Route</li>

<li>Patient instructions</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Forms of Medication</h2>


<div class="card">


<table>


<tr>

<th>Form</th>

<th>Description</th>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Tablet</td>

<td>Solid medication taken orally.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Capsule</td>

<td>Medication enclosed in a dissolvable shell.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Solution</td>

<td>Medication completely dissolved in liquid.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Suspension</td>

<td>Medication particles mixed in liquid and must usually be shaken.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Cream/Ointment</td>

<td>Medication applied to the skin.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Patch</td>

<td>Medication absorbed through the skin.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Suppository</td>

<td>Medication inserted into a body cavity.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Inhaler</td>

<td>Medication delivered through the respiratory system.</td>

</tr>


</table>


</div>
